  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1245 AM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nStorms haven't really panned out very well considering the consensus \namong CAMS for the last 24 hours. Warn on forecast still indicates \nsome development along and southeast of highway 14 over the next \nhour or two so we could still be dealing with warnable storms \nthrough the early morning hours. Again, large hail and high winds as \noutlined in the previous discussions/updates are the chief concern.  \n\nAnd we'll try to do it all again later today. Flow remains \nnorthwesterly aloft, with a subtle trough going across the Dakotas \n03-09Z. Not much different, with strong unidirectional shear \noverall, but weak mid level warm advection and light low level flow \nat/off the surface. 1/2km winds are only about 15-20kts out of the \nsoutheast. CAMS are much less organized and more inconsistent in \nplacement of individual convection. Thus, much lower confidence in \nregards to POPs (ie timing & coverage). \n\nStill looks to be a nice shift into cooler temperatures for the \nlatter half of the work week though overall the upper level flow \nregime remains northwesterly. A ridge builds in from the west Friday \nas an upper high shifts from 4-corners centric to southeast CONUS, \nwhich may then retrograde back to the west next week. Overall, flow \nremains active and its hard to support the elimination of NBM \nspammed POPs for any one specific period.  \n\nIn regards to smoke, not much overall change. Flow aloft will \ncontinue to support high smoke streaming across the region and \nhelping to suppress temperatures slightly. Low level flow around the \ncooler high pressure system mid-week may present a risk but smoke \nwould have to take a circuitous route as boreal/northern Canada \ndoesn't appear to be a source region anymore.",
